#import "../prelude.typ": *

#definition(title: "Brauer group")[
  Let $k$ be a field and $k_s$ be one of its separable closures. The #term[Brauer group] of $k$ is
  $
    "Br"(k) := H^2(k,k_s)
  $
  If $K|k$ is a separable subextension of $k_s|k$ we can define the #term[relative Brauer group] of the extension as
  $
    "Br"(K|k) := ker("Br"(k) -->^"Res" "Br"(K))
  $
]

The elements of $"Br"(k)$ correspond to the finite dimensional division algebras $D$ with center $k$. In this correspondence, the subgroup $"Br"(K|k)$ gives those $D$ for which $D times.o_k K tilde.eq M_(n times n)(K)$, where $n^2 = dim_k D$.

#lemma[
  1. If $K|k$ is a Galois extension with group $G$ then it holds $"Br"(K|k)tilde.eq H^2(G,K^times)$;
  2. The Brauer group $"Br"(k)$ is the union of all the subgroups $"Br"(K|k)$ for finite Galois extensions $K|k$;
  3. If $G$ is finite and cyclic it holds $"Br"(K|k) tilde.eq k^times / (N_(K|k)(K^times))$.
]<lemma:brauer>

Here $N_(K|k) : K^times -> k^times$ denotes the norm of the field extension. When $K|k$ is finite and Galois it has value
$
  N_(K|k)(a)=product_(sigma in "Gal"(K|k)) sigma a
$
for every $a in K^times$.

#proof[
  1. From #link(<missing-ref>)[Hilbert's theorem 90] we have $H^1(G,K^times)=0$. As seen in #ref(<prop:restriction-inflation-sequence>) this makes the following inflation-restriction sequence exact: $
    0 --> H^2(G,K^times) -->^"Inf" H^2(k,k_s) -->^"Res" H^2(K,k_s),
  $ and by definition $"Br"(K|k)= ker "Res"=H^2(G,K^times)$.
  2. From the previous point we deduce $
    "Br"(k) = lim_--> H^2(G,K^times) =^((1)) lim_--> "Br"(K|k)= union.big "Br"(K|k).
    $
  3. As seen in #ref(<example:cyclic-cohomology>) when the Galois group $G$ is cyclic we have $
    "Br"(K|k) =^((1)) H^2(G,K^times)=(K^times)^G / (N_(K|k)(K^times)) = k^times / (N_(K|k)(K^times)).
    $
]

#example[
  0. When the field $k$ is complete we have $"Br"(k) = 0$ since the Galois action on it is trivial.
  1. Let's compute the Brauer group of the real numbers: $
    "Br"(RR) = "Br"(CC|RR) = H^2("Gal"(CC|RR),CC^times) = RR^times / (N_(CC|RR)(CC^times)) = RR^times / RR^times_(>0) tilde.eq ZZ\/2ZZ.
  $
  2. When $k = FF_q$ is a finite field the Brauer group vanishes: $"Br"(FF_q) = 0$. By #ref(<lemma:brauer>) it is enough to show that $"Br"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q) = 0$ for every $r > 0$. Recall the following facts:
    - The group $"Gal"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q)$ is cyclic, generated by the frobenius $a |-> a^q$. In particular the norm has value $
      N(a)=a^((q^r-1)/(q-1));
    $
    - The multiplicative group $FF_(q^r)^times$ is cyclic.
    Let $omega$ be a generator of $FF_(q^r)^times$, its norm $N(omega)$ by the above calculation has order $q-1$ and therefore generates $FF_q$. This shows $N$ is surjective and from #ref(<lemma:brauer>) we deduce $"Br"(FF_(q^r)|FF_q) = 0$.
]

== The path to $K|QQ_p$

The main goal of this section is proving that if $K|QQ_p$ is a $p$-adic number field then its Brauer group is $"Br"(K) = QQ \/ ZZ$.

#definition(title: "Discretely valuated fields")[
  A #term[discretely valuated field] is a field equipped with a discrete valuation $v : K -> ZZ union {infinity}$, i.e. it holds
  - $v(x) = infinity <==> x = 0$;
  - $v(x y) = v(x) v(y)$;
  - $v(x + y) >= min {v(x), v(y)}$.
  Moreover we say $K$ is #term[complete] if it complete with respect to the norm $||x||_v = e^(-v(x))$.
  The #term[valuation ring] of $K$ is defined as $
    cal(O)_K := {x in K : v(x) >= 0},
  $
  it is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal $M_K := {x in K : v(x) > 0}$.
  The #term[residue field] of $K$ is the quotient $k(v) := cal(O)_K \/ M_K$.
]

#new-theorem(kind: "fact", numbering: false)[
  The ideal $M_k$ is principal and it is generated by any element $pi in K$ with valuation $v(pi) = 1$, such an element is called a #term[uniformiser] for $cal(O)_K$. Since $M_K$ is maximal, $k(v) = cal(O)_K \/ (pi)$ is indeed a field.
]

#new-theorem(kind: "fact", numbering: false)[
  The field $K$ is complete if and only if its valuation ring $cal(O)_K$ is a complete DVR, that is $
    cal(O)_K tilde.eq lim_(<--_i) cal(O)_K / M_K^i.
  $
]

#example[
  1. The $p$-adic field $QQ_p$ is complete as $QQ_p = "Frac"(lim_<- ZZ \/ p^i ZZ)$.
  2. The Laurent series $k((t))$ form a complete field as $k((t)) = "Frac"(lim_<- k[|t|] \/ (t^i))$.
]

#new-theorem(kind: "fact", numbering: false)[
  If $(K,v)$ is a complete discretely valuated field and $L|K$ is a finite separable extension, the valuation can be extended uniquely to $L$ via $
    w = 1 / ([L:K]) v compose N_(L|K): L^times -> 1 / ([L:K]) ZZ ->^tilde ZZ.
  $
  The discretely valuated field $(L,w)$ is complete itself and its valuation ring $cal(O)_L$ is the integral closure of $cal(O)_K$ inside $L$.
]

#definition(title: "ramification and residue class")[
  If $L|K$ is a finite separable extension of complete discretely valuated fields, the associated #term[ramification index] is $e(L|K) := [w(L^times):v(K^times)]$ and the #term[residue class degree] is $f(L|K) := [k(w):k(v)]$.

  We say $L|K$ is #term[unramified] if $k(w)|k(v)$ is separable and $e = 1$; and $L|K$ is #term[totally ramified] if $f = 1$.
]

From now on we assume that $K$ is a complete discretely valuated field.

#new-theorem(kind: "facts", numbering: false)[
  - When $k(w)|k(v)$ is separable it holds $[L:K] = e(L|K) dot f(L|K)$.
  - If $L|K$ is finite and unramified there exists a unique extension $L supset M supset K$ such that $M|K$ is unramified and $L|M$ is totally ramified.
  - _Towers property:_ If both $L|K, L'|L$ are finite and unramified, $L'|K$ is also finite and unramified.
  - _Shift property:_ If $L|K$ is finite and unramified, $K'|K$ is finite and separable; the extension $L K'|K'$ is finite and unramified.
]

#definition(title: "Unramified closure")[
  We set the maximal unramified extension of $K$ as $
    K_"nr" = union.big \{overline(K) supset L supset K : L|K "is finite and unramified"\}.
  $
]

The maximal unramified extension $K_"nr"$ is a dicretely valuated field, with valuation extending that of $K$. Its residue field is the separable closure $k(v)_s$, however in general it is not a complete field.

Suppose $L|K$ is a finite Galois extension (this can be generalised to infinite Galois extensions) and let $G = "Gal"(L|K)$. The action of $G$ on $L$ is compatible with the valuation (this is a consequence of the uniqueness of valuation extensions), meaning $cal(O)_L$ and $M_L$ are $G$-invariant. Every element $sigma in G$ induces an automorphism
$
  overline(sigma) : cal(O)_L / M_L -> cal(O)_K / M_K, wide overline(x) |-> overline(sigma(x)),
$
giving a homomorphism on the Galois group of the residue fields extension
$
  rho : "Gal"(L|K) -> "Gal"(k(w)|k(v)).
$
By #ref(<prop:hensel-lemma>) this is surjective, moreover its kernel is given by the #term[inertia subgroup] $I(L|K)$ and it is known that $abs(I(L|K)) = e(L|K)$. In particular when the extension is unramified (i.e. $e = 1$) the map $rho$ is an isomorphism.

Notice that by a similar argument if $L|K$ is a finite unramified extension then $L|K$ is Galois if and only if the residue field extension $k(w)|k(v)$ is Galois.

#example[
  Suppose the residue field $k(v)$ is finite. Since finite extension on finite fields are Galois, every finite unramified extension $L|K$ is a cyclic Galois extension. This means that for every $n | [L:K]$ there is a unique unramified subextension $L supset K_n supset K$ of degree $[K_n:K]=n$. In other words if we fix a closure $overline(K)$ for every $n$ there is a unique unramified Galois extension $overline(K) supset K_n supset K$ of degree $n$.
]

#definition(title: "Units and principal units groups")[
  The #term[units group] of $K$ is the subgroup $cal(U)_K := {a in K^times : v(a) = 0}$. The #term[$i$-th group of principal units] is $cal(U)_K^((i)) := {a in K^times : v(a - 1) >= i}$.
]

There is an exact sequence of groups
$
  1 --> cal(U)_K --> K^times -->^v ZZ --> 1,
$
the units group can be interpreted as the kernel of the valuation map. Moreover there is a surjection $cal(U)_K ->> k(v)^times$ given by $u |-> u mod M_K$ and the principal units group can be viewed as its kernel:
$
  cal(U)_K^((1)) = ker (cal(U)_K --> k(v)^times).
$
Notice that $inter.big_i cal(U)_K^i={1 + a in K^times : v(a) >= i "for each" i >= 1} = {1}$.
The map $cal(U)_K^((i)) -> M_K^i$ given by $u |-> u mod M_K^(i+1)$ induces an isomorphism
$
  cal(U)_K^((i)) / cal(U)_K^((i+1)) -->^tilde M_K^i / M_K^(i+1) tilde.eq (pi^i cal(O)_K) / (pi^(i+1) cal(O)_K) tilde.eq cal(O)_K / ((pi)) tilde.eq k(v).
$
We have therefore constructed a filtration (consisting of an infinite number of elements)
$
  cal(U)_K supset_(k(v)^times) cal(U)_K^((1)) supset_(k(v)^plus) cal(U)_K^((2)) supset_(k(v)^plus) dots supset {1},
$
this filtration can be useful to prove properties of the multiplicative group $K^times$.

#example[
  Consider a finite $p$-adic extension $K|QQ_p$. The residue field $k(v)$ is a finite extension of $FF_p$, hence it is of the form $k(v) = FF_(q)$ where $q = p^r, r = [K:QQ_p]$. The exact sequence
  $
    1 --> cal(U)_K^((1)) --> cal(U)_K --> k(v)^times -> 1
  $
  splits by lifting $k(v)^times$ to the $q - 1$ roots of unity in $cal(U)_K$ via $k(v)^times -> mu_(q-1)$. This gives the decomposition
  $
    K^times tilde.eq ZZ times cal(U)_K tilde.eq ZZ times mu_(q-1) times cal(U)_K^((1)).
  $
  It can be shown that $cal(U)_K^((1))$ itself can be decomposed as $cal(U)_K^((1)) tilde.eq mu_(p^s) times ZZ_p^r$, where $p^s$ is the number of $p$-power roots of unity in $K$.

  We deduce $K^times \/ K^(times n)$ is a finite abelian group.
]

== The Hasse invariant

The goal of this section is to prove the Hasse theorem on Brauer groups of complete discretely valuated fields with finite residue field. In particular, this applies to finite $p$-adic extensions $K|QQ_p$. We then proceed to use this result to define the Hasse invariant map, that underlies the generalization of the Hilbert symbol.
#text(fill: red)[to be updated.]

The main result is that in the discussed hypothesis $"Br"(K) tilde.eq QQ \/ ZZ$. We divide the proof in two steps: we first prove $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq QQ \/ ZZ$, secondly we show $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) = "Br"(K)$.

From now on let $K$ be a complete discretely valued field with finite residue field $kappa$.

=== Showing $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq QQ \/ ZZ$

#lemma[
  Let $L|K$ be a finite unramified Galois extension and let $G = "Gal"(L|K)$. There is a split short exact sequence
  $
    0 --> H^2(G,cal(U)_L) --> H^2(G,L^times) --> "Hom"(G,QQ \/ ZZ) --> 0.
  $
] <lemma:unit-cohomology>

#proof[
  The short exact sequence
  $
    1 --> cal(U)_L --> L^times -->^v ZZ --> 0
  $
  splits via $s : ZZ -> L^times, 1 |-> pi$ where $pi$ is a uniformiser of $cal(O)_L$.
  Since the extension is unramified, we can choose $pi in K$, so that the section $s$ is $G$-equivariant. Thus $s$ induces a map on cohomology (that we still denote $s$) and the exact sequence
  $
    0 = H^1(G,ZZ) --> H^2(G,cal(U)_L) --> H^2(G,L^times) --> H^2(G,ZZ) -->  0
  $
  also splits via the section $s : H^2(G,ZZ) -> H^2(G,L^times)$ (indeed, the existence of the section guarantees surjectivity of the right-hand map).
  It remains to justify that $H^1(G,ZZ) = 0$: since the $G$-action on $ZZ$ is trivial we have $H^1(G,ZZ)="Hom"(G,ZZ)$ and every homomorphism from a finite group to $ZZ$ is trivial.

  It remains to identify $H^2(G,ZZ)$ with $"Hom"(G,QQ\/ZZ)$. Consider the short exact sequence
  $
    0 --> ZZ --> QQ --> QQ \/ ZZ --> 0,
  $
  the associated long sequence in cohomology gives
  $
    0 = H^1(G,QQ) --> H^1(G,QQ \/ ZZ) -->^tilde H^2(G,ZZ) --> H^2(G,QQ) = 0.
  $
  From #ref(<cor:divisible-trivial-cohomology>) the cohomology of $QQ$ vanishes in positive dimension. Since the $G$-action on $QQ \/ ZZ$ is trivial we have $H^1(G,QQ\/ZZ) = "Hom"(G,QQ\/ZZ)$ and we conclude.
]

#lemma[
  Let $G$ be a finite group and let $(A_j,phi_j)$ be an inverse system of $G$-modules indexed by $ZZ_(>0)$ (in particular the transition maps are the $phi_j : A_(j+1) -> A_j$). For any $i$, if $H^i (G,A_1) = 0$ and $H^i (G, ker phi_j) = 0$ for every $j$, then $H^i (G, lim_<- A_j) = 0$.
] <lemma:cohomology-inverse-limit>

#lemma(title: "Additive Hilbert's 90")[
  If $K$ is a field and $L|K$ is a finite Galois extension with group $G$, then $H^i (G,L^+) = 0$ for every $i > 0$.
] <lemma:hilbert-90-additive>

In particular from #ref(<lemma:cohomology-inverse-limit>) we deduce $H^i (k,k_s) = 0$ for every $i > 0$.

#proof[
  Recall the _normal basis theorem_: there is an element $a in L$ such that the set ${sigma a : a in G}$ forms a $k$-basis for $L$. We deduce there is an isomorphism of $G$-modules
  $
    k^+ times.o_ZZ ZZ[G] -> L^+, wide lambda_i times.o sigma_i |-> lambda_i sigma_i(a).
  $
  Moreover there is a non-canonical isomorphism $k^+ times.o_ZZ ZZ[G] tilde.eq "Hom"_ZZ (ZZ[G],k^+)$ of $k$-vector spaces since they have the same (finite) dimension. The latter is the coinduced-$G$-module $M^G (k^+)$ and therefore by #ref(<lemma:shapiro>) we conclude $H^i (G, M^G (k^+)) = 0$.
]

#proposition[
  Let $L|K$ be a finite unramified Galois extension with Galois group $G$. Let $kappa$ and $lambda$ denote the residue fields of $K$ and $L$, respectively. The reduction map $cal(U)_L -> lambda^times$ induces an isomorphism $H^2(G,cal(U)_L) ->^tilde H^2(G,lambda^times)$.
] <prop:unit-reduction-isomorphism>

#proof[
  The quotients $cal(U)_L^((1)) \/ cal(U)_L^((j))$ form an inverse system of groups and since the principal units groups $cal(U)_L^((j))$ have trivial intersection we get an isomorphism $cal(U)_L^((1)) tilde.eq lim_<- cal(U)_L^((1)) \/ cal(U)_L^((j))$.
  For every $j >= 1$ there is an exact sequence
  $
    1 --> cal(U)_L^(j+1) --> cal(U)_L^j --> lambda^+ --> 1 ==> lambda^+ tilde.eq cal(U)_L^((j)) \/ cal(U)_L^((j+1)).
  $
  For every $i > 0$, by #ref(<lemma:hilbert-90-additive>) we have $H^i (G,lambda^+) = 0$ and therefore $H^i (G, cal(U)_L^((j)) \/ cal(U)_L^((j+1))) = 0$. Applying #ref(<lemma:cohomology-inverse-limit>) yields $H^i (G,cal(U)_L^((1))) = 0$.
  Now extract the cohomology long exact sequence from $1 -> cal(U)_L^((1)) -> cal(U)_L -> lambda^times -> 1$, we get
  $
    0 = H^2(G,cal(U)_L^((1))) --> H^2(G,cal(U)_L) -->^tilde H^2(G,lambda^times) --> H^3(G,cal(U)_L^((1))) = 0,
  $
  that is the isomorphism we desired.
]

#corollary[
  We have a split short exact sequence
  $
    0 --> "Br"(lambda|kappa) --> "Br"(L|K) --> "Hom"(G,QQ\/ZZ) --> 0.
  $
  In particular when $kappa$ is finite, if we let $n = [L:K]$, then $"Br"(L|K) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$.
] <cor:brauer-sequence-finite>

#proof[
  By #ref(<lemma:unit-cohomology>) and #ref(<prop:unit-reduction-isomorphism>) we get the desired sequence.
  When $kappa$ is finite, since the extension $lambda|kappa$ is itself finite (recall that $"Gal"(lambda|kappa) tilde.eq "Gal"(L|K)$ in our case) we have $"Br"(lambda|kappa) = 0$. Moreover $n = [L:K] = abs(G)$ and therefore $"Hom"(G,QQ\/ZZ) = ZZ\/n ZZ$.
]

We can now apply the direct limit over all finite unramified Galois extension $L|K$ to the short exact sequence and get the following result:

#theorem(title: $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq QQ\/ZZ$)[
  If $K$ is a complete dicretely valuated field with perfect residue field $kappa$, then we have a split short exact sequence
  $
    0 --> "Br"(kappa) --> "Br"(K_"nr"|K) --> "Hom"_"cont" ("Gal"(overline(kappa)|kappa),QQ\/ZZ) --> 0.
  $
  If moreover $kappa$ is finite we get $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq QQ\/ZZ$.
] <thm:unramified-brauer-group>

=== Showing $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq "Br"(K)$

This fact is in general true for any complete discretely valuated field, however we are only going to prove it for fields $K$ with a finite residue field.

#lemma[
  If $K$ is a complete discretely valuated field and $L|K$ is a finite Galois extension with Galois group $G$, then the units group $cal(U)_L$ contains a $G$-submodule $V_L^1 subset cal(U)_L$ of finite index such that $H^i (G,V_L^1) = 0$ for every $i > 0$.
]

#proof[
  From the _normal basis theorem_ there is an $a in L$ such that the set ${sigma a : sigma in G}$ is a $k$-basis of $L$. Let $pi$ be a uniformiser of $cal(O)_K$ (i.e. $v_K (pi) = 1$), since $G$ is finite there is a $r >> 0$ such that $pi^r sigma a in cal(O)_L$ for every $sigma in G$. Define the $cal(O)_K$-module $M = chevron.l pi^r sigma a : sigma in G chevron.r_(cal(O)_K)$, it is a submodule of $cal(O)_L$. We know $cal(O)_L$ is free of rank $[L:K]$ and by construction this also holds for $M$. By the structure theorem of finitely generated modules on PIDs the quotient $cal(O)_L \/ M$ is finite (since it is torsion and finitely generated). This shows that $pi^m cal(O)_L subset M$ for a suitable $m$, fix such an $m$. Define $V_L^j := 1 + pi^(m+j) M subset cal(U)_L$ and consider the map
  $
    V_L^j -> M / (pi M), wide 1 + pi^(m+j) a |-> a mod pi M.
  $
  Its kernel is exactly $V_L^(j+1)$, therefore the map induces an isomorphism
  $
    V_L^j / V_L^(j+1) -->^tilde M / (pi M) tilde.eq cal(O)_K / ((pi)) times.o ZZ[G] tilde.eq kappa times.o ZZ[G].
  $
  By #ref(<lemma:shapiro>), the cohomology group $H^i (G,kappa times.o ZZ[G]) = 0$ since $kappa times.o ZZ[G]$ is isomorphic to a coinduced-module.
  Moreover $V_L^1 tilde.eq lim_<- V_L^1 \/ V_L^j$ and by #ref(<lemma:cohomology-inverse-limit>) we get $H^1(G,V_L^1) = 0$.

  Finally, $V_L^1 subset cal(U)_L$ has finite index since it contains the subgroup $1 + pi^(2 m + 1) cal(O)_L$, which has finite index in $cal(U)_L$.
]

#corollary[
  The cohomology group $H^i (G,cal(U)_L)$ is finite for every $i > 0$.
]

#proof[
  By the short exact sequence $1 -> V_L^1 -> cal(U)_L -> cal(U)_L \/ V_L^1 -> 1$ we extract the cohomology long exact sequence
  $
    0 = H^i (G, V_L^1) --> H^i (G,cal(U)_L) -->^tilde H^i (G, cal(U)_L \/ V_L^1) --> H^(i+1) (G, V_L^1) = 0,
  $
  Indeed, by the previous lemma $H^i (G, V_L^1) = 0$ and the quotient $cal(U)_L \/ V_L^1$ is finite.
]

#lemma[
  Let $G$ be a finite cyclic group and let $A$ be a finite $G$-module. We have
  $
  abs(H^1(G,A)) = abs(H^2(G,A)).
  $
] <lemma:finite-cyclic-cohomology>

#proof[
  We have computed the cohomology of a cyclic group in #ref(<example:cyclic-cohomology>):
  $
   H^1(G,A)= attach(A, bl: N) / ((sigma - 1) A), wide H^2(G,A) = A^G / (N A),
  $
  where $sigma$ is a generator of $G$ and $N = sum_(sigma in G) sigma$.
  We have two short exact sequences
  $
    0 --> A^G --> A --> (sigma - 1) A --> 0 \
    0 --> attach(A, bl: N) --> A --> N A --> 0
  $
  from which we deduce $abs(A^G) abs((sigma - 1) A) = abs(A) = abs(attach(A, bl: N)) abs(N A)$.
]

#proposition[
  If $K$ is a complete discretely valuated field and $L|K$ is a finite Galois extension with cyclic Galois group $G tilde.eq ZZ \/ n ZZ$, then the relative Brauer group has order $abs("Br"(L|K))=n$.
]

#proof[
  We need to show that $H^2(G, L^times)$ is finite of order $n$. From the short exact sequence $1 -> cal(U)_L -> L^times -> ZZ -> 1$ we extract the cohomology long exact sequence
  $
    0 = H^1(G,ZZ) --> H^2(G,cal(U)_L) --> H^2(G,L^times) --> H^2(G,ZZ) --> H^3(G,cal(U)_L) --> H^3(G,L^times) = 0.
  $
  Indeed since $G$ is finite $H^1(G, ZZ) = 0$ and since $G$ is also cyclic $H^3(G,L^times) = H^1(G,L^times) = 0$ by #link(<missing-ref>)[Hilbert's 90].
  Since all the elements in the sequence have finite order we deduce
  $
    abs(H^2(G, L^times)) abs(H^3(G, cal(U)_L)) = abs(H^2(G, cal(U)_L)) abs(H^2(G,ZZ)) ==>^(#ref(<lemma:finite-cyclic-cohomology>)) abs(H^2(G, L^times)) = abs(H^2(G,ZZ)).
  $
  Since $H^2(G,ZZ) tilde.eq "Hom"(G,QQ\/ZZ) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$ we conclude. 
]

#proposition[
  If $L|K$ is a finite separable extension the following diagram commutes:
  $
    #text(fill: red)[diagram]
  $
] <prop:brauer-restriction-degree>

#proof[
  Let $L supset M supset K$ be the unique middle extension such that $L|M$ is totally ramified and $M|K$ is unramified. We prove the theorem separatedly for totally ramified and unramified extensions, then since both $"Res"$ and the rank satisfy the tower property we conclude it must hold for all $L|K$.

  In both cases we are going to use the following fact: if $G$ is a profinite group, the long group cohomology sequence extracted from $0 -> ZZ -> QQ -> QQ\/ZZ -> 0$ gives
  $
    0=H^1(G,QQ) --> H^1(G,QQ\/ZZ) -->^tilde H^2(G,ZZ) --> H^2(G,QQ)=0.
  $
  The isomorphism follows from #ref(<cor:divisible-trivial-cohomology>)
  - #boxed[$L|K$ is unramified] In particular we have $L_"nr" = K_"nr"$. Since the restriction map $"Res"$ is functorial (#exercise) the following diagram commutes:
    $
      "Br"(K_"nr"|K) = H^2("Gal"(K_"nr"|K),K_"nr"^times) -->^v H^2("Gal"(K_"nr"|K),ZZ) tilde.eq H^2("Gal"(overline(kappa)|kappa),ZZ) tilde.eq H^1(kappa, QQ\/ZZ)\
      #text(fill: red)[diagram]
    $
    #text(fill: red)[^here $H^1(kappa,QQ\/ZZ)=H^1("Gal"(overline(kappa)|kappa),QQ\/ZZ)$, right?]
    Moreover, since $QQ\/ZZ$ is a trivial module we get
    $
      H^1(kappa,QQ\/ZZ) = "Hom"(kappa,QQ\/ZZ) tilde.eq "Hom"(hat(ZZ),QQ\/ZZ) tilde.eq QQ\/ZZ.
    $
    The isomorphism $"Gal"(overline(kappa)|kappa) tilde.eq hat(ZZ)$ is obtained by sending $1$ to the Frobenius map $x |-> x^(|kappa|)$.
    Analagously $"Gal"(overline(kappa)|lambda) tilde.eq hat(ZZ)$ sends $1$ to $x |-> x^(|lambda|)$, hence the restriction map under this isomorphism is
    $
      [x |-> x^(|kappa|)] mapsto.long [x |-> x^(|lambda|) = x^(|kappa| [lambda:kappa])],
    $
    which becomes multiplication by $[lambda:kappa]$ on $QQ\/ZZ$. Since the extension is unramified #text(fill: red)[(is this why?)] $[lambda:kappa] = [L:K]$.
  - #boxed[$L|K$ is totally ramified] In particular $lambda = kappa$ and $L$ is linearly disjoint from $K_"nr"$ #text(fill: red)[(the intersection $L inter K_"nr"$ is both totally ramified and unramified, hence it must be $K$ itself)].
    Therefore we have $L_"nr" = L K_"nr"$ and $"Gal"(L_"nr"|L) tilde.eq "Gal"(K_"nr"|K)$. Consider once more the commutative diagram
    $
      #text(fill: red)[diagram]
    $
    Indeed, since the extension is totally ramified #text(fill: red)[(is this why?)] $v_L (K_"nr"^times) = [L:R] v_K (K_"nr"^times)$ and restricting to the group itself induces the identity map $"Res" = id$.
]

#theorem(title: $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq "Br"(K)$)[
  Let $K$ be a complete discretely valuated field with finite residue field. We have $"Br"(K_"nr"|K) tilde.eq "Br"(K)$.
] <thm:unramified-equals-standard-brauer-group>

#proof[
  Let $K_n|K$ be the unique unramified extension of $K$ of degree $n$ (this is indeed unique: from Galois correspondence unramified extensions of $K$ correspond $1:1$ to extensions of $kappa$, and for $kappa$ finite there is a unique degree $n$ extension). Let $L|K$ be any finite Galois extension of degree $n$, we shall prove $"Br"(L|K) = "Br"(K_n|K)$ as subgroups of $"Br"(K)$. This concludes as $"Br"(K_"nr"|K)$ is the union of all the $"Br"(K_n|K)$ and $"Br"(K)$ is the union of all the $"Br"(L|K)$ #text(fill: red)[(is this precise? write it in a better way)].
  - #boxed[$"Br"(K_n|K) subset "Br"(L|K)$] Consider the commutative diagram
    $
      #text(fill: red)[diagram]
    $
    From #ref(<prop:brauer-restriction-degree>) the restriction map is in fact multiplication by $[L:K] = n$. Since $"Gal"(K_n|K) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$ from #ref(<cor:cohomology-torsion>) we deduce $[L:K] dot "Br"(K_n|K) = 0$. Its image in $"Br"(L_"nr"|L) subset "Br"(L)$ is therefore trivial and this shows it is contained in the kernel $"Br"(L|K)$.
  We now resort to showing $"Br"(L|K)$ is finite and has order dividing $n$. Since it contains $"Br"(K_n|K) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$ this shows their equality #text(fill: red)[(why? Could it not be $ZZ\/n ZZ times ZZ\/n ZZ$ for example?)].
  We prove it by steps:
  - #boxed[$L|K$ is cyclic] From #ref(<cor:brauer-sequence-finite>) we get $"Br"(L|K) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$.
  - #boxed[$"Gal"(L|K)$ is a $p$-group] Let $n = p^r$. Since $p$-groups are solvable there is a chain of subfields
    $
      K = L_0 subset L_1 subset dots subset L_r = L
    $
    such that $L_i|K$ is Galois and $"Gal"(L_i|L_(i-1)) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ$ for each $i$.
    The inflation-restriction sequence (cfr. #ref(<prop:restriction-inflation-sequence>)) yields
    $
      0 --> "Br"(L_i|L_(i-1)) -->^"Inf" "Br"(L_i|K) -->^"Res" "Br"(L_(i-1)|K).
    $
    The group $"Br"(L_i|L_(i-1))$ has order $p$ and by induction $"Br"(L_(i-1)|K)$ has order dividing $p^(i-1)$, hence $"Br"(L_i|K)$ has order dividing $p^i$. We get $|"Br"(L|K)| divides p^r$.
  - #boxed[General case] Let $n = p_1^r_1 dots p_m^r_m$. The Brauer group decomposes as
    $
      "Br"(L|K) = plus.o.big_(i=1)^m "Br"(L|K)[p_i].
    $
    For each $i$ fix a $p_i$-Sylow $P_i$ inside $"Gal"(L|K)$. Apply $"Res"$ and $"Cor"$ to $"Br"(L|K)[p_i]$ with respect to the subgroup $P_i$, we get
    $
      "Br"(L|K)[p_i] = H^2("Gal"(L|K),L^times)[p_i] -->^"Res" H^2(P_i, L^times) -->^"Cor" H^2("Gal"(L|K),L^times).
    $
    By #ref(<prop:cor-res-composition>) we have $"Cor" compose "Res" = [G:P_i]$ and since this is coprime with $p_i$ the composite map is injective. In particular $"Res"$ itself is injective. We obtain
    $
      "Br"(L|K) = plus.o.big_(i=1)^m "Br"(L|K)[p_i] arrow.r.hook plus.o.big_(i=1)^m H^2(P_i,L^times) = plus.o.big_(i=1)^m "Br"(L|K_i),
    $
    where $K_i = L^(P_i)$. By the previous case each component $"Br"(L|K_i)$ has order dividing $p_i^r_i$ and therefore $|"Br"(L|K)| divides n$.
]

#theorem(title: "Hasse")[
  Let $K$ be a complete discretely valuated field with finite residue field $kappa$. There is a canonical isomorphism $"inv" : "Br"(K) ->^tilde QQ\/ZZ$ called the #term[hasse invariant].
  Moreover, if $L|K$ is a finite separable extension the following diagrams commute:
  $
    #text(fill: red)[diagrams]
  $
] <thm:hasse>

#proof[
  The isomorphism $"inv"$ is obtained by #ref(<thm:unramified-brauer-group>) and #ref(<thm:unramified-equals-standard-brauer-group>).
  The first commutative diagram is #ref(<prop:brauer-restriction-degree>).
  It remains to show the second diagram commutes.
  Since the multiplication map $[L:K] : QQ\/ZZ -> QQ\/ZZ$ is surjective, the restriction map $"Res" : "Br"(K) -> "Br"(L)$ is surjective. Let $alpha in "Br"(L)$, there is an element $beta in "Br"(K)$ such that $alpha = "Res"(beta)$. Moreover, we have $"Cor"(alpha) = "Cor" compose "Res"(beta) = [L:K] beta$. We get
  $

    "inv"(alpha) = "inv"("Res"(beta)) =^((*)) [L:K] "inv"(beta) = "inv"("Cor"(alpha)),
  $
  where $(*)$ follows from the first diagram.
]

#corollary[
  If $(n,"char" K) = 1$ then $H^2(K,mu_n) = ZZ\/n ZZ$.
] <cor:brauer-roots-of-unity>

#proof[
  From the exact sequence
  $
    1 --> mu_n --> K_s -->^n K_s --> 1
  $
  we extract the long exact sequence in cohomology
  $
    0 = H^1(K,K_s^times) --> H^2(K,mu_n) --> H^2(K,K_s) -->^n H^2(K,K_s).
  $
  From #ref(<thm:hasse>) the Brauer group is $H^2(K,K_s) tilde.eq QQ\/ZZ$, hence
  $
    H^2(K,mu_n) tilde.eq ker(QQ\/ZZ -->^n QQ\/ZZ) tilde.eq ZZ\/n ZZ.
  $
]

== The generalised Hilbert symbol

#definition(title: "Hilbert symbol")[
  Let $(n,"char" K) = 1$. The #term[Hilbert symbol] is defined as the map
  $
    H^1(K,ZZ\/n) & times & H^1(K,mu_n) & --> H^2(K, mu_n times.o ZZ\/n), \
    chi &, & beta & mapsto.long chi smile beta
  $
]

#new-theorem(kind: "remark", numbering: false)[
  From Kummer theory we can identify $H^1(K,mu_n) tilde.eq K^times \/ K^(times n)$. Moreover, from #ref(<cor:brauer-roots-of-unity>) we can identify $H^2(K,mu_n times.o ZZ\/n) = H^2(K,mu_n) tilde.eq ZZ\/n$. In this sense the generalised Hilbert symbol can be interpreted as a map
  $
    H^1(K,ZZ\/n) times K^times\/K^(times n) --> ZZ\/n,
  $
  that pairs characters of the Galois group and elements of $K$.

  Moreover, when the roots of unity are contained in the field $mu_n subset K$ we can fix an isomorphism $mu_n tilde.eq ZZ\/n$ by choosing a root $omega in mu_n$. This gives the generalised Hilbert symbol an even closer resemblance to its original counterpart, as it becomes a map
  $
    K^times\/K^(times n) times K^times\/K^(times n) --> mu_n.
  $
  This is effectively a map that pairs two elements of $K$ with values in $mu_n$.
  Notice that by fixing the same isomorphism where we substituted $mu_n tilde.eq ZZ\/n$ the final map does not depend on the choice of $omega$.
]

#proposition[
  Suppose $chi in H^1(K,ZZ\/n ZZ)$ corresponds to a surjective character $"Gal"(K_s|K) ->> ZZ\/n ZZ$ (by eventually making $n$ smaller we can always find such a surjective character), let $L$ be the fixed field for $ker chi$. If $b in K^times$ then
  $
    (chi,b) = 1 <==> b in N_(L|K) (L^times).
  $
]

From this proposition we recover the classical Hilbert symbol, making the generalised Hilbert symbol effectively a generalisation of the former.
If $n = 2$ we have ${plus.minus 1} = mu_n subset K$ and $L = K(sqrt(a))$ for some $a in K$. We get
$
  (chi,b) = 1 & <==> b in N_(L|K) (L^times) \
  & <==> b = N(x + sqrt(a) y) = x^2 + a y^2 \
  & <==> x^2 + a y^2 - b z^2 = 0 "has a non-trivial solution"
$
that is exactly the definition of the classical Hilbert symbol when $K = QQ_p$.

#proof[
  
]